::PC_U02_L01_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U02_L01_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.1
Question:
If p(4)=0, which statement is true?
{
=A. x-4 is a factor of p(x)#Correct. If p(4)=0, then x-4 is a factor by factor reasoning.
~B. x+4 is a factor of p(x)#This sign is reversed; x+4 would correspond to p(-4)=0.
~C. 4 is the degree of p(x)#A function value of zero does not determine degree.
~D. p(x) has no zero#p(4)=0 means 4 is a zero.
}
::PC_U02_L01_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U02_L01_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.1
Question:
If p(2)=5, what is the remainder when p(x) is divided by x-2?
{
~A. 2#This is the input value, not the remainder.
=B. 5#Correct. The remainder is p(2), which is 5.
~C. 0#A zero remainder would mean x-2 is a factor.
~D. -2#This uses the opposite of the input and not the function value.
}
::PC_U02_L01_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U02_L01_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.1
Question:
Which zero corresponds to the factor x+9?
{
~A. 9#This uses the wrong sign.
~B. 0#The zero is not automatically 0.
=C. -9#Correct. Setting x+9=0 gives x=-9.
~D. 1#This value does not make the factor equal zero.
}
::PC_U02_L01_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U02_L01_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.1
Question:
What does a remainder of 0 indicate when dividing p(x) by x-c?
{
=A. x-c is a factor#Correct. A zero remainder means x-c divides the polynomial evenly.
~B. c is the leading coefficient#The remainder does not identify the leading coefficient.
~C. p(x) is constant#A zero remainder does not mean the polynomial is constant.
~D. x+c is always a factor#The sign and factor must match the tested value.
}
::PC_U02_L01_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U02_L01_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.1
Question:
If x=3 is a zero of a polynomial model, what can it mean in context?
{
~A. The y-intercept is 3#The y-intercept occurs at input 0.
~B. The maximum output is 3#A zero does not identify a maximum.
~C. The degree is 3#A zero does not determine the polynomial degree.
=D. The output is 0 when the input is 3#Correct. A zero means the modeled output equals 0 at that input.
}